{"name":"Flax Bloom","hex":"#d2d8f4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flax","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of the genus Linum, especially Linum usitatissimum, which has a single, slender stalk, about a foot and a half high, with blue flowers. Also known as linseed, especially when referring to the seeds.","The fibers of Linum usitatissimum, grown to make linen and related textiles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flax"},{"term":"bloom","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A blossom; the flower of a plant; an expanded bud.","Flowers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause to blossom; to make flourish.","To bestow a bloom upon; to make blooming or radiant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bloom"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flax","blocks":[{"text":"Flax, also known as common flax or line, is a flowering plant, Linum usitatissimum, in the family Linaceae. It is cultivated as a food and fiber crop in regions of the world with temperate climates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/K%C3%B6hler%27s_Medizinal-Pflanzen_in_naturgetreuen_Abbildungen_mit_kurz_erl%C3%A4uterndem_Texte_%28Plate_16%29_BHL303594.jpg/330px-K%C3%B6hler%27s_Medizinal-Pflanzen_in_naturgetreuen_Abbildungen_mit_kurz_erl%C3%A4uterndem_Texte_%28Plate_16%29_BHL303594.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flax","wikidata":"Q45108"},{"title":"Flax (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Flax or flaxen is a pale yellowish-gray, the color of straw or unspun dressed flax. The first recorded use of flax as a color name in English was in 1915, but flaxen had been used to describe hair color in David Copperfield, by Charles Dickens in 1849: Mr. Omer's granddaughter, Minnie, is described as \"a pretty little girl with long, flaxen, curling hair.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Vlasvezel.JPG/330px-